WEBVTT

00:00.867 --> 00:03.433
- Hey, Filipinos, where you at?

00:03.433 --> 00:05.100
Right here.

00:05.100 --> 00:06.467
Been here for a long time.

00:06.467 --> 00:09.133
(hip hop music)

00:19.300 --> 00:21.400
Filipino part of Seattle,

00:21.400 --> 00:24.967
specifically within
the Chinatown
International District.

00:25.867 --> 00:27.400
Does it still exist?

00:27.400 --> 00:29.100
Yeah, some people say it does,

00:29.100 --> 00:31.233
and that it deserves
to be recognized.

00:31.233 --> 00:33.533
Others claim that
it never existed,

00:33.533 --> 00:36.667
and there's been even
attempts to remove

00:36.667 --> 00:41.000
any language referring
to a Little Manila Town,

00:41.000 --> 00:42.600
or a Filipino Town.

00:42.600 --> 00:45.067
It's not like we
haven't been around.

00:45.067 --> 00:48.333
The 1903 Pensionado Act allowed

00:48.333 --> 00:51.800
for middle class Filipinos
in the Philippines

00:51.800 --> 00:54.633
to come to America to study
in American universities,

00:54.633 --> 00:57.300
and the Udub was
actually the place

00:57.300 --> 01:00.000
that had the highest
enrollment of Filipinos

01:00.000 --> 01:03.033
out of any university
in the country.

01:03.033 --> 01:05.833
The Manong Generation,
Filipinos coming in

01:05.833 --> 01:09.400
as migrant workers,
as cheap labor.

01:09.400 --> 01:13.800
Filipinos were at the forefront
of the labor organizing.

01:15.000 --> 01:17.500
They lived just east
of Pioneer Square

01:17.500 --> 01:20.533
that eventually
became the Chinatown
International District.

01:20.533 --> 01:24.167
If you go to the northwest
corner of Hing Hay Park,

01:24.167 --> 01:29.000
there's an old map, and that
map, it's a time capsule

01:29.000 --> 01:31.800
of what Chinatown idea was like.

01:31.800 --> 01:34.200
There was very much an ecosystem

01:34.200 --> 01:37.033
of a Filipino community
that was based

01:37.033 --> 01:40.733
in the Chinatown
International District

01:40.733 --> 01:42.767
around the '40s and '50s.

01:42.767 --> 01:47.000
The late Uncle Bob was
the unofficial mayor

01:47.000 --> 01:49.567
of Chinatown for many decades.

01:49.567 --> 01:52.800
The fact that the
neighborhood even still exists

01:52.800 --> 01:56.567
has a lot to owe
to that generation

01:56.567 --> 01:58.667
led by people like
Uncle Bob Santos.

02:00.267 --> 02:03.467
As with many communities,
things do change.

02:03.467 --> 02:06.333
Housing covenants slowly
started being removed

02:06.333 --> 02:09.833
from certain neighborhoods,
and Filipinos were now allowed

02:09.833 --> 02:12.833
to live and buy homes
in other neighborhoods.

02:12.833 --> 02:14.600
There was a definite
spreading out.

02:16.000 --> 02:19.933
You then had a generation
of these older Manongs

02:19.933 --> 02:21.933
who were still in the district,

02:21.933 --> 02:25.433
but little by little, some
shops would close down.

02:25.433 --> 02:29.133
I think about the
Massive Monkees Studio,

02:29.133 --> 02:31.933
and how a lot of second-
and third-generation

02:31.933 --> 02:35.000
Filipino-American kids
who are my age or younger

02:35.000 --> 02:36.400
are running that studio.

02:36.400 --> 02:38.933
A lot of them have
parents or grandparents

02:38.933 --> 02:42.933
whose entry point
was the Chinatown
International District,

02:42.933 --> 02:47.500
and now returning back in the
form of another generation

02:47.500 --> 02:50.933
doing this current
generation's art form

02:50.933 --> 02:53.700
for the next
generation of dancers.

02:53.700 --> 02:55.567
That is so dope to me.

02:55.567 --> 02:59.467
On the same block, you
got Moksha Boutique,

02:59.467 --> 03:03.767
which is run by Pinaya, a
Filipina-American entrepreneur.

03:05.200 --> 03:08.100
Then myself, I'm fortunate,
along with my wife Chera,

03:08.100 --> 03:09.467
we're gonna be opening

03:09.467 --> 03:12.633
the Hood Famous Bakeshop's
second location.

03:12.633 --> 03:15.333
I think about my
kids, and their kids.

03:16.700 --> 03:18.867
When they're going
around the city,

03:18.867 --> 03:22.667
will they be able to say,
"This is the neighborhood

03:22.667 --> 03:25.100
"that my community,
that my people,

03:25.100 --> 03:28.500
"that my ancestors were a
part of and helped build?"

03:28.500 --> 03:32.700
Why this matters so
much to me is legacy,

03:32.700 --> 03:35.267
is that future generations
of Filipinos know

03:35.267 --> 03:38.300
that they are a part of
the fabric of this place.
